ID: Ch11b-Clones
TEXT:

"How did you know we'd be here?" Kate asks, her boots shifting against the cold cobbles.

"You have your friend over there to thank," the green-robed mage says, pointing a gloved finger toward Rose. "A beggar overheard her telling a nurse at the hospital that she was bringing a healer for the ward. He sold us the tip for a handful of copper, and we paid off the city watch to clear the square in case the healer was the one we wanted. Zack, stay back. I don't need help dealing with weaklings. I can finish them myself."

"No, you can't," Daren says, stepping past the empty market stalls. "We already know your god trades in illusions. Phantoms are useless against people expecting them."

"Are you sure about that, healer?" The green-robed mage smiles, and in an instant, hundreds of identical copies of both mages flood the marketplace.

They stand shoulder to shoulder between the shuttered shops and canvas awnings, filling every alley and stone doorway in unbroken, silent ranks.

"You haven't moved an inch, genius," Daren says, lunging straight forward. "I know which one is real."

Daren reaches the spot where the two stood and swings his broadsword in a wide, vicious arc. The heavy steel cleaves through both men, but neither bleeds. They dissolve into gray mist that curls around his blade and vanishes.

"What is this?" Kate says, frost crackling faintly across her knuckles. "If those two were illusions, were the real casters hidden under an invisibility veil the whole time?"

"Arraka," I say, glancing down at Flower's chest, "why didn't you warn us about invisible mages?"

"Because nobody is invisible," Arraka snaps from the gold locket. "If anyone were cloaking their aura, I would have caught the ripple."

"Then how were the two standing in front of us phantoms?" I ask.

"How am I supposed to know, jackass? Figure it out yourself!"

"Can you at least tell which of these duplicates are the originals?" I ask.

"No," Arraka says. "I never bothered learning parlor tricks for sorting clones. Back in my day, if someone pulled this garbage on me, I dropped a spell that flattened the entire district at once. End of problem."

"Does anyone know why none of these clones are moving?" Hadrik asks, resting heavy hands on his belt. "They're just standing there smirking like fools. Are they waiting on something?"

choice("Ask Kate if she can freeze the whole area around us, to take care of the clones.", Ch11b-Freeze, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Freeze)
choice("Try to concentrate on finding the originals, and ignore the clones for now.", Ch11b-Concentrate, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Concentrate)


ID: Ch11b-Freeze
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_premonition < 4)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_premonition >= 4)
#if(v_premonition < 4) {
 

"Kate," I say, "can you freeze the whole square? Take out every clone at once."

"I was about to," Kate says, stepping forward with palms outstretched.

She thrusts both hands forward, releasing a massive ring of frost that tears across the cobblestones, shattering every duplicate in a single blinding burst of ice. The moment the illusions vanish, the frost wave violently reverses course, rebounding inward before anyone can brace.

The others weather the blast with shields or natural resilience, but the cold hits my chest like a warhammer. My blood freezes in my veins. My breath locks in my throat, and my pulse slows to a faint, dying flutter.

"Kate, thaw him!" Daren shouts. "Quickly!"

"I can't!" Kate's voice cracks in panic. "I don't know how!"

Daren drops to his knees beside me, white light flaring frantically from his gauntlets, but the warmth never reaches my core. My vision tunnels into black. I slip into the dark without ever learning what turned Kate's magic back on us.

}
#if(v_premonition >= 4) {
 

"Kate," I say, "can you freeze the whole square? Take out every clone at once."

Before the words leave my tongue, a jagged premonition tears through my mind: Kate blasting the square with frost, the illusions popping like bubbles, and the wave snapping back to crush my chest in a lethal rebound.

"I was about to," Kate says, raising her hands.

"Wait!" I shout, catching her forearm. "Don't!"

Kate hesitates, lowering her palms just as frost bites the air.

"What is it?" she asks, glancing at me.

"A premonition," I say, catching my breath. "If you unleash that frost, the spell bounces back and hits us the instant the clones pop."

"So the duplicates are a tripwire?" Hadrik asks, stepping closer.

"Hold on," Arraka says from the amulet. "I've got it."

"You recognize the spell?" Kate asks.

"Of course I do," Arraka says. "It's one of Memphir's favorite parlor tricks. No surprise he taught it to his lackeys."

"Memphir?" Hadrik asks. "The God of Fate?"

"That's the one," Arraka says. "The spell is rigged so that when the illusions break, whatever magic killed them reflects straight back into the caster. I used to walk into that stupid trap all the time."

"How are you sure it's the same construct?" I ask.

"Look at their auras," Arraka says. "There's a telltale wobble in the weave. A slithering ripple, like a snake gliding through tall grass. See it?"

"Yeah," I say, focusing on the nearest duplicate. "I see it."

"Hey, I see it too!" Hadrik says, grinning. "Perfect! We just look for the bodies without that wobble, and those are the originals!"

"Uh-huh," Arraka says dryly. "Good luck with that."

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_premonition < 4)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_premonition < 4)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_premonition < 4)
choice("Continue", Ch11b-Square, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Square) if (v_premonition >= 4)


ID: Ch11b-Concentrate
TEXT:

"I'll freeze the entire square," Kate says, raising her palms. "Wipe out every clone at once."

"No, wait," I say, holding up a hand. "We should focus on finding the originals first."

"You think breaking the clones triggers a trap?" Kate asks, fingers still curled with frost.

"I don't know," I say, scanning the motionless rows between the market stalls. "But they aren't attacking. They're waiting for us to commit. While they're standing still, let's figure out the trick before we throw magic into it."

"Hold on," Arraka says from the amulet. "I've got it."

"Did you find the originals?" Kate asks.

"No, but I know what construct they're using," Arraka says. "It's one of Memphir's favorite parlor tricks. No surprise he taught it to his lackeys."

"Memphir?" Hadrik asks. "The God of Fate?"

"That's the one," Arraka says. "The spell is rigged so that when the illusions break, whatever magic killed them reflects straight back into the caster. I used to walk into that stupid trap all the time."

"How are you sure it's the same construct?" I ask.

"Look at their auras," Arraka says. "There's a telltale wobble in the weave. A slithering ripple, like a snake gliding through tall grass. See it?"

"Yeah," I say, locking my gaze onto the duplicate nearest the fountain. "I see it."

"Hey, I see it too!" Hadrik says, grinning. "Perfect! We just look for the bodies without that wobble, and those are the originals!"

"Uh-huh," Arraka says dryly. "Good luck with that."

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Square, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Square)


ID: Ch11b-Square
TEXT:

"Why do you say that?" Kate asks.

"Because I already scanned them," Arraka says. "Twice. Not a single one is real."

"Are you saying the actual mages never came here?" Daren asks, lowering his broadsword a fraction. "That they cast this from across the city?"

"No," Arraka says. "I've been watching this sector since the streets went quiet. The only casters close enough to anchor an illusion were the two who stepped out."

"That doesn't add up," I say. "If those two were real, where did they go? Did they turn themselves into illusions when the spell triggered?"

The stillness of the square feels wrong. The shuttered windows, the deserted awnings, the silent duplicates standing frozen in the morning light.

"Wait..." I say. "Illuna, check my theory. Could this entire scene be an illusion? Not just the clones, but the streets, the buildings, everything? Like the shaman's smoke ritual in the fort."

"Impossible," Illuna says through Flower's lips. "A mind-affecting construct on that scale requires a sensory anchor, the way the underground caves filled with smoke. There was no—"

Illuna cuts herself off.

"The green flash," she says, her voice tightening. "Of course. I should have seen it immediately. That explains why Rose collapsed. The mind-affecting weave couldn't latch onto her under tournament law, so inside the hallucination, her body fell unconscious. And the same happened to Flower."

"Great," Daren says, grip tightening on his hilt. "Back to square one. We need to find the real mages, kill them, and break the construct."

"No," Illuna says. "The shaman locked himself inside the illusion to keep us trapped. These mages aren't here with us. This construct was designed to stall our minds while they butcher our bodies in the waking world. Time is running at the same speed as reality."

"Gods," Kate says, her face going pale. "Flower is fighting both of them alone out there!"

"Close your eyes," Illuna commands. "Visualize the street exactly as it was before the green flash. Hold the image with absolute conviction that it is the real world and you are returning to it. Do it together, and the illusion shatters."

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Honor, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Honor)


ID: Ch11b-Honor
TEXT:

We close our eyes and anchor our minds to the memory of the sunlit cobblestones. The image sharpens, pulling hard against the mental fog until the construct gives way with a sharp, ringing snap. When I open my eyes, the marketplace is wrecked.

Deep craters scar the pavement. Stone walls are scorched black, and the air reeks of ozone and burnt sulfur. In the center of the street stands a massive dragon made of blackened bone. Rose is on her knees, weeping against a shattered cart, and Flower is barely upright, swaying on battered feet while glaring at the beast. Her clothes are torn, her skin is covered in dark bruises, but an intense crimson aura radiates from her frame, burning with heat fiercer than any elemental flame. Sensing our return, she glances back with a weary smile.

"You broke it..." she murmurs. "I knew you would..."

Her eyes flutter shut, and she collapses into the dust. Daren sprints forward, slamming his heavy shield down between her fallen body and the dragon.

"She wouldn't stop," Rose says through tears, wiping soot from her cheeks. "She kept fighting long after her strength ran out. She took every hit just to keep the dragon off you..."

"She's breathing," Illuna says, taking control of the limp body without standing up. "The girl passed out from sheer exhaustion. She isn't dying."

"Look what your incompetence bought us, fool!" Zack the necromancer snarls at his partner. "If your useless god's spell had worked, we wouldn't be in this mess!"

"Don't put this on me!" the green-robed mage snaps, drenched in sweat and trembling from magical depletion. "The construct wasn't built for fused souls. It captured the dominant mind and left the other free! You're the one hoarding your stillwater summons like a miser. If you'd called them when I told you to, they'd already be corpses!"

"Hold your tongue, heathen," Zack spits. "That invocation is permitted only once a day. Wasting it on morning warm-ups would insult the God of Death!"

"Are you insane?!" the green-robed mage yells, throwing his arms wide. "Do you have any idea how much mana it took to sustain that construct? I dropped all my personal wards two hours ago just to hold the feed! I don't even have a basic shield up right now!"

Mid-shout, a cone of jagged ice punches straight through the center of his forehead. He drops face-first onto the stones, dead before he hits the ground.

"Thanks for letting me know," Kate says, lowering her hand without looking at him.

"Wench!" Zack roars, trembling with rage. "Have you no honor?!"

"It's his own fault for dropping his guard in a fight," Kate says coldly. "And don't talk to me about honor. You would have slaughtered us in our sleep if Flower hadn't held the line."

"Silence, wench!" Zack snarls. "Honor is wasted on irredeemable heathens like you!"

choice(""How do you know that we're irredeemable?"", Ch11b-Irredeemable, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Irredeemable)
choice(""Wasn't your friend a heathen too, though?"", Ch11b-Heathen, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Heathen)
choice(""If Ulruk only wants the already-damned, he doesn't need followers. He needs a census."", Ch11b-Interviews, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Interviews)


ID: Ch11b-Irredeemable
TEXT:

"How do you know that we're irredeemable?"

"Reaching adulthood without swearing your life to the God of Death is proof enough, heathen!" Zack says, raising his staff. "Enough prattle. I will show you firsthand why every breath you took before today was a mistake."

"Illuna, can you move?" Daren asks, glancing back over his shoulder.

"No," Illuna rasps through Flower's bruised lips. "The girl pushed her body far past its limits in the waking world. Every muscle and bone is screaming. I can't lift a finger. She's battered from head to toe, her clothes are shredded, and Arraka's amulet is..."

She halts. A sharp, metallic snap echoes from the gold around her neck.

"Oh..." Illuna breathes. "No..."

"Aha—AHAHAHA—"

The amulet fractures again, splitting down the center with a violent, reverberating crack. Arraka's laughter spills out, layering into dozens of shrieking voices that echo off the stone walls.

"AHAHAHAHAHAHAHA!"

The gold bursts into glittering shards. A suffocating dark vortex erupts around Flower's body, lifting her upright into midair. Gale-force winds slam into our chests, driving us back across the stones as the sky darkens to the bruised color of a storm. Flower's eyes shift from bright blue to absolute, lightless black. A predatory grin stretches across her face as she looks down at us.

"So this is what you look like in the flesh!" Arraka says, her voice echoing as several women speaking in unison. "Hard to appreciate the scenery from inside a golden box going by aura alone. How you walk around every day without being hauled into a traveling circus is beyond me."

"You're one to talk," Hadrik says, planting his boots against the gale. "You do realize you're literally dressed like a clown right now?"

Arraka's black eyes fix on the dwarf, her grin widening.

"Dwarf," she purrs. "Is cracking jokes really the best move right now? Shouldn't you be buying that round of ale you owe me? I could use a drink. That and mass slaughter. I haven't decided which to start with."

"Flower..." Kate's voice trembles as frost falters at her wrists. "What happened to Flower? Where are Flower and Illuna?"

"Dead, obviously," Arraka says smoothly. "Or 'absorbed,' if you prefer technical terms. The moment the box cracked, I consumed their souls to anchor the vessel. They belong to me now."

"So they're still in there... somewhere?" Kate asks.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Monster, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Monster)


ID: Ch11b-Heathen
TEXT:

"Wasn't your friend a heathen too, though? Why would there have been any need for honor when killing him, then?"

"Because he was serving the God of Death's design!" Zack roars. "You, on the other hand, are so far beyond salvation that your only purpose is fertilizer for his glory. It is my sacred duty to send you to him before you taint the earth any further."

"Illuna, can you move?" Daren asks, glancing back over his shoulder.

"No," Illuna rasps through Flower's bruised lips. "The girl pushed her body far past its limits in the waking world. Every muscle and bone is screaming. I can't lift a finger. She's battered from head to toe, her clothes are shredded, and Arraka's amulet is..."

She halts. A sharp, metallic snap echoes from the gold around her neck.

"Oh..." Illuna breathes. "No..."

