ID: Ch11b-Interviews
TEXT:

"If Ulruk only wants the already-damned, he doesn't need followers. He needs a census."

"Do not mock the almighty God of Death, heathen!" Zack spits. "Ulruk welcomes all who surrender their lives to his service, without exception. If you are useless to him breathing, you will serve him in death. And that service begins now."

"Illuna, can you move?" Daren asks, glancing back over his shoulder.

"No," Illuna rasps through Flower's bruised lips. "The girl pushed her body far past its limits in the waking world. Every muscle and bone is screaming. I can't lift a finger. She's battered from head to toe, her clothes are shredded, and Arraka's amulet is..."

She halts. A sharp, metallic snap echoes from the gold around her neck.

"Oh..." Illuna breathes. "No..."

"Aha—AHAHAHA—"

The amulet fractures again, splitting down the center with a violent, reverberating crack. Arraka's laughter spills out, layering into dozens of shrieking voices that echo off the stone walls.

"AHAHAHAHAHAHAHA!"

The gold bursts into glittering shards. A suffocating dark vortex erupts around Flower's body, lifting her upright into midair. Gale-force winds slam into our chests, driving us back across the stones as the sky darkens to the bruised color of a storm. Flower's eyes shift from bright blue to absolute, lightless black. A predatory grin stretches across her face as she looks down at us.

"So this is what you look like in the flesh!" Arraka says, her voice echoing as several women speaking in unison. "Hard to appreciate the scenery from inside a golden box going by aura alone. How you walk around every day without being hauled into a traveling circus is beyond me."

"You're one to talk," Hadrik says, planting his boots against the gale. "You do realize you're literally dressed like a clown right now?"

Arraka's black eyes fix on the dwarf, her grin widening.

"Dwarf," she purrs. "Is cracking jokes really the best move right now? Shouldn't you be buying that round of ale you owe me? I could use a drink. That and mass slaughter. I haven't decided which to start with."

"Flower..." Kate's voice trembles as frost falters at her wrists. "What happened to Flower? Where are Flower and Illuna?"

"Dead, obviously," Arraka says smoothly. "Or 'absorbed,' if you prefer technical terms. The moment the box cracked, I consumed their souls to anchor the vessel. They belong to me now."

"So they're still in there... somewhere?" Kate asks.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Monster, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Monster)


ID: Ch11b-Monster
TEXT:

"Well, if you want to be pedantic about it," Arraka says, waving a dismissive hand, "I suppose you could say—"

"Monster!" Zack shrieks, pointing a trembling finger at her. "Abomination! Begone, foul demon!"

The skeletal dragon, motionless since our return, rears back with a ground-shaking roar and lunges straight for Arraka, jaws gaping wide. The instant its skull brushes her outer aura, the entire construct detonates into thousands of jagged bone fragments that rain across the marketplace like hail.

"Really, now..." Arraka murmurs, brushing a flake of bone from her shoulder.

The cobblestones shudder beneath our boots. The earth heaves, cracking open as five humanoid skeletons claw their way upward from the dirt. The icy, dense pressure in their auras gives them away immediately: stillwaters.

"Do you see now, banshee?" Zack cries, spreading his arms wide. "Do you see the true majesty of the God of Death? Fall to your knees and beg Ulruk for mercy, and he might grant you a quick end!"

Arraka ignores the necromancer entirely, floating lower to squint at the center skeleton.

"Wait a second," she says, grinning. "I know you! Short-legs Vallen! The one who couldn't carry a tune if his life depended on it. You aren't going to try hurting me, are you? Don't you remember all the good times during the Still Winter? Remember when we had to drag you out of the pond because you tried practicing levitation and forgot you couldn't swim? Remember how we laughed at your waddling after you drained your mana? Remember when I tied you to that oak and made you watch me dismantle your entire squad one by one until you hit the high notes on that nursery rhyme about the lamb? Those were the days. How did it go again?"

Arraka clears her throat and belts out a shrill, mocking tune that echoes through the empty square.

"Oh, there was a lamb, a little white lamb, with fleece as bright as snow!"

The skeleton named Vallen violently recoils. It clutches its bony skull with both hands, trembling as if phantom agony were splitting its brain. The tremor turns to frenzy; it throws back its head in a soundless scream and unleashes twin torrents of white-hot flame from its palms. The fire splashes against Arraka's passive barrier like water on glass. She doesn't flinch, carrying the melody through the roar of flames.

"Attack her, you fools!" Zack roars at the other skeletons. "Kill her!"

The remaining four unleash everything at once: gale-force shears, stone spikes, lances of lightning, and dispelling curses designed to shred protective wards. Arraka strolls through the tempest of elemental magic without missing a beat, singing her nursery rhyme at the top of her lungs.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Sonic, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Sonic)


ID: Ch11b-Sonic
TEXT:

"Drop the act, banshee!" Zack yells over the magical roar. "You can't fool me! You're barely holding your barrier against their assault, and you have no way to strike back. My skeletons are warded against sonic attacks—they're indestructible! You can't touch them!"

Arraka stops singing. Her pitch-black eyes lock onto the necromancer with a slow, venomous smirk.

"Warded against sound?" she says, mocking. "How resourceful. Truly, a mastermind of the arcane arts. Tell me, necromancer—did you think to ward your puppets against pebbles?"

"What kind of idiocy is—" Zack starts.

Arraka doesn't let him finish.

With a flick of her wrist, half a dozen loose stones lift from the dirt into her palm. She snaps her fingers, flicking the pebbles forward like crossbow bolts. The moment a pebble strikes a skeleton, the entire frame explodes—not into shards, but into clouds of gray powder so fine it hangs like smoke in the morning air.

Zack's jaw falls slack. The color drains from his face, leaving him frozen in shock.

"Be a dear, sweep that up, and send it to old Ulruk with my compliments," Arraka says, gesturing toward the heaps of dust. "Tell him it's from Arraka. Tell him to expect plenty more deliveries soon. Once I'm finished cleaning up the earthen plane, I'm paying him a visit. Him, Memphir, and every other self-important god who thought banning banshees from the magical plane was funny. We'll have ourselves a lovely reunion party!"

"Shut up!" Daren steps forward, broadsword drawn, face rigid with fury. "I don't care about your ancient feuds. Bring them back. Flower and Illuna. Return them this instant!"

Arraka tilts her head. "Excuse me?"

"You heard me," Daren says, his voice dropping dangerous and low. "Give them back their body."

"Are you deaf or just stupid?" Arraka sneers. "I told you, I absorbed them. What do you expect me to do, carve them out with a kitchen knife?"

"If you won't release them, I'll cut them out myself," Daren says, raising his blade. "Prepare yourself, banshee!"

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Murder, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Murder)


ID: Ch11b-Murder
TEXT:

"Mass slaughter it is," Arraka says, her grin splitting wide across Flower's stolen face.

Daren charges. Three strides in, the air twists violently around his frame. The flesh on his face and hands instantly blackens, shriveling like parched parchment in a kiln. In a single heartbeat, the body inside his armor collapses to ash, and heavy steel plate crashes loudly onto the cobblestones.

"No..." Kate's voice strangles in her throat. "No!"

Her hands snap forward, conjuring a storm of frost and a hundred jagged icicles aimed at Arraka's throat—and then the same dark rot touches her. The ice shatters into vapor. Kate's skin turns gray, desiccating in an instant, and her green cloak drops limp over a mound of pale dust.

"Stop!" Rose screams, dropping to her knees on the stones, weeping wildly. "Please! Stop!"

"What's wrong, Hadrik?" Arraka taunts, black eyes gleaming. "Where's that legendary giant fighting spirit you love to brag about? I'm standing right here. What are you waiting for?"

"I'll show you fighting spirit, you arrogant old hag," Hadrik snarls. Sweat beads on his brow, but he lowers his shoulder and charges, heavy dwarven boots pounding the pavement.

Mid-stride, the dark rot takes him. His battle roar cuts off into silence. The dwarf disintegrates into gray powder, his leather tunic and belt collapsing into the dirt.

"Please..." Rose whispers, hands clutching the stones, broken.

They are gone. In three seconds, three lives erased. Disbelief locks my chest. This has to be another illusion. It must be. The shaman's trick again.

I squeeze my eyes shut, clawing for the memory of the real street. I force every ounce of will into visualizing reality, trying to throw myself back into the waking world—but nothing shifts. The cold wind stays. The smell of ash and ozone stays.

"You aren't seriously closing your eyes and pretending I'm not here, are you?" Arraka calls down, amused. "Draw your dagger, Barry. It's no fun executing you if you don't even twitch."

choice("Take out my dagger and attack Arraka", Ch11b-Dagger, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Dagger)
choice(""I'm not going to give you that satisfaction."", Ch11b-Satisfaction, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Satisfaction)


ID: Ch11b-Satisfaction
TEXT:

"I'm not going to give you that satisfaction."

"Hmph," Arraka says, floating closer. "I was planning to draw your death out to torment your little friend, but if you won't even struggle, what's the point?"

She casually flicks her hand toward Rose. The dark rot hits her in a heartbeat, reducing the weeping herbalist to a mound of pale ash.

"Well, it was an entertaining run while it lasted, Barry," Arraka says, hovering inches away. "Your road ends here, but don't worry—you'll always have a fond place in my memories."

She raises her hand toward my chest. A searing dryness grips my veins, sucking every drop of moisture from my flesh. My vision blurs into gray static as my knees buckle onto the stones.

"Farewell, Barry," Arraka purrs. "It's been a blast."

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: Ch11b-Dagger
TEXT:

I draw my dagger and lock eyes with the banshee.

"Now that's the spirit!" Arraka laughs, eyes flashing pitch black. "An epic duel for the ages. Hero against villain. Banshee against lessathi. Bards will compose ballads about this clash for millennia. Let's see what you've got!"

Standing here mourning does nothing. She is arrogant, floating without a guard, treating me like vermin. If I reach her, if I drive cold steel through this vessel, maybe the magic breaks. Maybe killing her unravels the rot and brings the others back. There is only one way to find out.

I sprint across the cobblestones, blade raised—and a heavy paving stone slams into my solar plexus at terrifying speed, knocking me flat onto my back.

"Oh dear, was that too rough?" Arraka mocks as I wheeze, gasping for air on the stone. "Pardon me, I've never fought a fragile weakling before. I don't know your breaking point!"

"Leave him alone!" Rose screams through her tears.

"I wouldn't want to end this too quickly," Arraka says, hovering overhead. "A proper duel requires pacing, wouldn't you say?"

Another stone hammers into my floating ribs with a sickening crack. The breath tears out of my lungs in a ragged, blood-choked gasp.

"Stop!" Rose cries, crawling toward me. "You're killing him!"

"Honestly," Arraka sighs, shaking her head. "I knew you were frail, Barry, but this is pathetic. It's painful to watch. Let's just put you out of your misery."

"No!" Rose shrieks at the top of her voice. "Don't do it!"

"Goodbye, Barry!" Arraka waves a casual hand, and the freezing, desiccating rot grips my chest, shriveling the skin across my hands. "It's been fun!"

"STOP!"

Rose's voice tears through the market square, raw and jagged with grief, cracking the air like thunder.

The rot halts.

The wind dies instantly. The dust freezes mid-drift. A solitary tear hangs suspended an inch below Rose's cheek, glinting in the morning light. Arraka's vessel hangs motionless in the sky, her black eyes locked in a static sneer.

Is this what happens at the threshold of death? Does time stretch to infinity when your heart stops? I have never died before.

Then the world snaps backward.

Like a tapestry unraveling, events reel in reverse before my eyes. The stone lifts from my bruised ribs and flies back to the street. The ash on the stones draws upward, knitting together into Hadrik's charging form, then Kate's conjuring stance, then Daren's armored charge. The dust of the stillwater skeletons gathers into bone, the skeletal dragon reassembles, and Arraka's body sinks toward the cobblestones as the golden amulet repairs itself, sealing shut around Flower's neck.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Weaver, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Weaver)


ID: Ch11b-Weaver
TEXT:

The rewinding timeline flashes through Flower trading blows with the dragon in the street, craters vanishing beneath cobblestones that heal themselves, all the way back to the instant the two mages sprang their ambush.

The reverse rush slams to a dead halt.

For a second, the world holds still at the exact moment after the ambush sprang. Then motion snaps back. Sound rushes into my ears like a breaking dam. Everyone is standing in place, looking around the empty marketplace with wide, dazed eyes.

Time reversed. And every single one of us remembers the deaths.

Before anyone can speak, Arraka detonates in fury from the golden amulet.

"A time weaver?!" she shrieks, voice rattling the metal casing. "Are you out of your minds?! Rose, of all people?! You knew! You set me up! You let me break out just to make a fool of me! I am done playing nice! The next time I get out, I'm ripping every bone from your bodies and making your families watch! You will beg for the clean deaths I gave you today! Stop grinning at me, you fat dwarf!"

"Be quiet, Arraka," Illuna says coldly. "I don't know what happened while you were out, but the context is obvious. You lost. We won. One more word and I seal this amulet shut for twenty years."

The amulet goes silent, vibrating with muffled rage.

"I'm... alive?" the green-robed mage stammers, checking his uninjured hands in disbelief. "How?"

"The woman!" Zack points a shaking finger at Rose. "I saw it with my own eyes. She rewound time! Do you understand what this means? What the gods would pay to harvest that power?!"

Before the green-robed mage can reply, an ice cone punches clean through his forehead. He collapses into the dust, stone dead.

"Your partner shouldn't have announced that he dropped all his wards two hours ago," Kate says coldly, lowering her hand. "Begging for a spear through the skull."

"I don't care about that worthless lackey," Zack says, stepping over the corpse. "Hand over the woman peacefully, and I'll grant you swift deaths."

"Tempting offer," Hadrik says, grinning broadly, "but we'll pass."

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Safety, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Safety)


ID: Ch11b-Safety
TEXT:

"Have it your way!" Zack snarls, raising his hands to summon the stillwater skeletons.

"Not this time!" Hadrik bellows, charging forward to crush him before the incantation finishes. But a skeleton, clawing its upper torso out of the dirt, unleashes a concussive blast of wind that hurls the dwarf twenty feet back into the square.

"Rose, fall back!" Daren shouts. "The tournament spell shields you from targeted attacks, but crossfire will tear you apart! Get clear!"

"Understood!" Rose sprints toward the far edge of the marketplace, ducking behind a row of empty fruit stands.

The five stillwater skeletons pull themselves free from the cobblestones. Two immediately rain streams of fire and pressurized water across the street, while a third raises a shimmering dome of arcane force protecting the squad. Daren plants his broadsword, anchoring a white radiant barrier around us with his heavy shield, but the elemental barrage hammers the light so violently the ground shakes.

"If anyone has a plan, speak up," Daren grunts, sweat pouring down his forehead as two massive stone slabs slam against his barrier from either side. "This won't hold."

"We can't trade brute force with five stillwaters," Illuna says. "Find their weaknesses. They're skeletons. Their physical vessels are brittle. Look for an opening."

"Hurry it up, then," Daren says through clenched teeth. "I give this barrier thirty seconds, tops."

Illuna is right. Undead vessels leak power and lack living resilience. Even if the necromancer shielded them against sonic vibrations, their constructs have limits. Besides, the summon lasts only five minutes and burns his daily use—survive the timer, and the fight is over.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Emergency, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Emergency)


ID: Ch11b-Emergency
TEXT:

Studying the five stillwaters through Daren's barrier, the flaw in their summon becomes obvious. Their auras carry that same bottomless, icy pressure as Eiden's, but their physical vessels are failing. Raw mana bleeds continuously from their dry joints in shimmering waves—their brittle bones simply cannot contain that volume of power.

I pull the stat device from my belt.

No spare points, but the screen pulses with a sharp amber alert in the lower corner: 'Emergency mode available. Tap here to activate.'

The device is siphoning the leaking stillwater overflow, exactly as it did in the forest before the dragon fight. There is no time to analyze the mechanism. I tap the screen.

Power floods into my veins like liquid lightning. For a split second, two hidden categories flicker onto the display—'Magical power' and 'Magical knowledge'—before dissolving into the active interface.

Spell matrices snap into focus behind my eyes. Fire, water, wind, earth, acid, lightning. It is a narrower, rougher arsenal than Eiden's divine infusion—no true flight spell, no instantaneous blink—meaning aerial movement requires wrestling raw updrafts manually. But the destructive output is real.

I have five minutes of borrowed power. The question is how to use it: step out and fight alone with maximum firepower, or coordinate with the party so we don't trip over one another.

choice("Fight the skeletons alone", Ch11b-Alone, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Alone)
choice("Fight the skeletons as a team", Ch11b-Team, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Team)


ID: Ch11b-Alone
TEXT:

I have never trained in squad-level magical coordination. Weaving high-tier spells around allies in a narrow cobblestone street is an invitation to incinerate my own friends. I fight alone.

Five stillwaters stand before us: four dedicated elementalists, and one support specialist weaving protective domes and reinforcing wards. Their discipline suggests an old Still Winter combat unit that perished together on the line.

They have immense reserves, but they are not Eiden. They are bound by ordinary casting rules—single-element affinity, telegraphed incantations, standard mana paths. My device gives me access to every elemental discipline at once.

The question is collateral. If I call down meteors and wide-area storms, the shockwaves will level half the street. The marketplace is cleared, but families are huddled behind closed shutters in the surrounding homes.

choice("Ignore the civilians. Cast all of my strongest spells.", Ch11b-Strongest, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Strongest, v_ch11_strategy = 1)
choice("Only cast the spells that would not run the risk of hurting civilians.", Ch11b-Civilians, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Civilians, v_ch11_strategy = 2)


ID: Ch11b-Team
TEXT:

Trading blows with five stillwaters alone is reckless when I have seasoned fighters at my side. Coordinated pressure gives us the tactical edge.

Five stillwaters stand before us: four dedicated elementalists, and one support specialist weaving protective domes and reinforcing wards. Their discipline suggests an old Still Winter combat unit that perished together on the line.

They have immense reserves, but they are not Eiden. They are bound by ordinary casting rules—single-element affinity, telegraphed incantations, standard mana paths. My device gives me access to every elemental discipline at once.

The question is our objective. If we aim to run the clock on the necromancer's five-minute summon, we reinforce Daren's shield and snipe the support mage to prevent him from weakening our wards. If we aim to destroy the stillwaters outright, we target the necromancer directly to force the skeletons into a desperate defensive scramble.

choice("Try to buy time until the spell runs out", Ch11b-Buy, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Buy, v_ch11_strategy = 3)
choice("Try to eliminate the stillwaters directly", Ch11b-Directly, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Directly, v_ch11_strategy = 4)


ID: Ch11b-Strongest
TEXT:

"I have a plan," I say. "Stay inside the barrier. I'll reinforce it from the outside and deal with the skeletons myself."

"What in the world?" Daren stares at me, eyes wide. "Where is that aura coming from? Did Eiden stop time again?"

"No," I say, channeling mana into Daren's shield to harden the weave. "The device is siphoning the mana leaking from their bones."

"It can do that?" Daren asks, glancing at the glowing screen.

"Apparently," I say. "Hold this perimeter."

I weave a dense shell of kinetic and elemental wards around myself, take a breath, and step outside the glowing dome.

I open with raw destruction. Gathering mana above the rooftops, I drag a burning meteor down through the clouds, slamming it directly into the stillwaters' position. The impact detonates with an ear-splitting roar, blowing the facades off two neighboring buildings in a storm of pulverized brick. When the smoke clears, their support mage's barrier is still glowing, the skeletons untouched.

I press the assault without a breath. I summon a howling blizzard over the square, lacing the gale with jagged bolts of lightning, while twin hands of solid granite erupt from the street to crush the barrier between massive stone palms.

Through the chaos, I loose a lance of lightning at the necromancer, but the support mage reacts in a fraction of a second, snapping a secondary ward into place to ground the strike.

A ripple in the air warns me too late. The air elementalist teleports directly in front of my face, slamming two skeletal palms into my chest. A point-blank gale blast throws me backward into a brick wall. I catch myself on an improvised updraft of wind, lifting into the air just as the earth elementalist raises two massive stone pillars to crush the spot where I landed.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Strongest2, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Strongest2)


ID: Ch11b-Strongest2
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch11_stillwater,1)
achievement("Stillwater slayer",v_ac_ch11_stillwater)
Riding raw wind currents without a true flight spell is clumsy and jarring, but it keeps me off the ground. Seeing me airborne, the air elementalist blinks back inside the safety of his squad's dome.

"No, idiot! You're doing it all wrong!" Arraka's voice screeches from the amulet below. "Stop hammering the entire perimeter and strike a single point! That support mage is pitifully slow at reinforcing localized punctures—he never learned concentrated mana distribution! I told him six hundred years ago, and the moron didn't believe me until I punched a tree branch clean through his ribs!"

Taking tactical advice from Arraka grates, but looking at the shimmering dome, I see the truth in her insult. The energy is spread wide across the sphere—resilient against broad area blasts, but paper-thin against a concentrated strike.

I circle overhead, dodging incoming fireballs and ice shards, while channeling a concentrated spear of lightning between my palms. When the voltage hums so violently the air turns to ozone, I line up the shot and fire straight at the support mage's skull.

The lightning spear punches through the barrier like a needle through parchment, blowing the support mage's skull into scorched splinters.

The dome shatters. The remaining four skeletons freeze in sudden confusion.

I seize the window. Pouring a massive surge of mana into the street, I transmute the cobblestones beneath them into a bubbling, steaming pool of corrosive acid.

The skeletons sink into the green slurry, their lower limbs dissolving on contact. The air elementalist conjures a panicked updraft to lift his upper torso out of the vat, but I clap my hands, slamming two granite slabs together to crush his skull to dust. The others submerge completely, their bones hissing and melting into sludge.

I drop back to the pavement as my device's power fades to zero. Blood drips from my nose, my hands shake violently from mana strain, and the acid pool dissolves into harmless mist, leaving Zack the necromancer standing alone in the ruins of the square.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Sacrificial, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Sacrificial)


ID: Ch11b-Civilians
TEXT:

"I have a plan," I say. "Stay inside the barrier. I'll reinforce it from the outside and deal with the skeletons myself."

"What in the world?" Daren stares at me, eyes wide. "Where is that aura coming from? Did Eiden stop time again?"

"No," I say, channeling mana into Daren's shield to harden the weave. "The device is siphoning the mana leaking from their bones."

"It can do that?" Daren asks, glancing at the glowing screen.

"Apparently," I say. "Hold this perimeter."

I cast a tight shell of kinetic and elemental protections around myself, take a breath, and step outside the glowing dome.

Without relying on wide-area bombardment, the priority is the summoner. If I pressure Zack, the stillwaters will break formation to protect their master.

The instant I clear the perimeter, I fire a concentrated lightning lance at the necromancer. The support mage anticipates the line, throwing a reinforced stone ward into the path of the bolt to ground the charge. The earth elementalist counters immediately, thrusting two stone walls inward to crush me, but I catch an updraft of wind and lift into the air above their reach.

Balancing on raw air currents is jarring, but it gives me elevation.

Hovering thirty feet above the square, I hammer the skeletons' dome with directed fire streams and cascading stone spikes. While their support mage strains to reinforce the ceiling, the fire and water elementalists merge their casts below, erupting a dense, boiling cloud of superheated steam that engulfs the street and swallows my position.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Civilians2, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Civilians2)


ID: Ch11b-Civilians2
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch11_stillwater,1) if (v_hearing >= 3 || v_perception >= 2)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_hearing >= 3 || v_perception >= 2)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_hearing < 3 && v_perception < 2)
achievement("Stillwater slayer",v_ac_ch11_stillwater)
#if(v_perception >= 2) {
 

Dense vapor blinds my vision, but faint sparks flicker in the mist right beside my shoulder. I bank hard on a sudden updraft, throwing myself out of the cloud.

A fraction of a second later, the sparks ignite into a massive concussive detonation. The shockwave catches my slipstream, sending me tumbling through the air before I stabilize on a fresh current.

"No, idiot! You're doing it all wrong!" Arraka's voice screeches from the amulet below. "Stop hammering the entire perimeter and strike a single point! That support mage is pitifully slow at reinforcing localized punctures—he never learned concentrated mana distribution! I told him six hundred years ago, and the moron didn't believe me until I punched a tree branch clean through his ribs!"

