ID: Ch11b-Buy
TEXT:

"I have a plan," I say.

"What in the world?" Daren stares at me, eyes wide. "Where is that aura coming from? Did Eiden stop time again?"

"No," I say, channeling mana into Daren's shield to harden the weave. "The device is siphoning the mana leaking from their bones."

"It can do that?" Daren asks, adjusting his grip.

"Apparently," I say. "Listen—Azarius told us the necromancer's summon lasts five minutes and burns his daily use. We run down the clock. We hold this perimeter and hammer them from range."

"Then you'd better have strong reinforcement weaves," Daren grunts, bracing his shield against the tremor. "My reservoir is bone dry."

"Focus on the anchor," I say. "I'll handle reinforcement."

I turn to Flower and Kate.

"Put pressure on their lines," I tell them. "Focus on the skeleton maintaining their dome—he's the one countering our wards."

"Understood!" Flower calls out, offering a quick salute.

"I'll find an opening," Kate says, ice forming along her knuckles.

"And what's my job?" Hadrik asks, cracking his knuckles.

"Last line of defense," I tell him. "If the barrier buckles before the timer, punch back whatever comes through."

"Terrific," Hadrik grunts. "Times like this make me wish I packed a deck of cards."

I pour mana into Daren's barrier while Kate, Flower, and Illuna pelt the stillwaters' dome with frost and fire. But holding against five coordinated stillwaters is grueling. Daren is shaking under the strain, and my borrowed reserves drain fast under the constant barrage. Across the street, the support mage efficiently repairs every fracture in their dome while actively eroding our light.

We won't make five minutes. The barrier is going to buckle.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Buy2, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Buy2)


ID: Ch11b-Buy2
TEXT:

"Hadrik," I call out, "change of plans. How do you feel about a distraction?"

The dwarf's face splits into a broad grin.

"Now you're talking, Barry!" He rolls his shoulders. "Want me to go give the necromancer a scare?"

"Exactly that," I say. "Hold still while I layer protections."

I wrap Hadrik in kinetic and heat wards. The instant the magic settles, he launches out of the dome like a cannonball, closing the distance in seconds. The air elementalist reacts with a gale blast, tossing Hadrik twenty paces back, but the dwarf scrambles up and charges right back, hammering the support barrier with heavy, concussive fists.

"No, idiot! You're doing it all wrong!" Arraka's voice screeches from the amulet below. "Stop hammering the entire perimeter and strike a single point! That support mage is pitifully slow at reinforcing localized punctures—he never learned concentrated mana distribution! I told him six hundred years ago, and the moron didn't believe me until I punched a tree branch clean through his ribs!"

Taking tactical advice from Arraka grates, but looking at the shimmering dome, I see the truth in her insult. The energy is spread wide across the sphere—resilient against broad area blasts, but paper-thin against a concentrated strike.

I channel a concentrated spear of lightning between my palms. When the voltage hums so violently the air turns to ozone, I line up the shot and fire straight at the support mage's skull.

The lightning spear punches through the barrier like a needle through parchment, blowing the support mage's skull into scorched splinters.

The dome shatters. The remaining four skeletons freeze in sudden confusion.

I seize the window. Pouring a massive surge of mana into the street, I transmute the cobblestones beneath them into a bubbling, steaming pool of corrosive acid.

The skeletons sink into the green slurry, their lower limbs dissolving on contact. The air elementalist conjures a panicked updraft to lift his upper torso out of the vat, but I clap hands, slamming two granite slabs together to crush his skull to dust. The others submerge completely, their bones hissing and melting into sludge.

I drop back to the pavement as my device's power fades to zero. Blood drips from my nose, my hands shake violently from mana strain, and the acid pool dissolves into harmless mist, leaving Zack the necromancer standing alone in the ruins of the square.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Sacrificial, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Sacrificial)


ID: Ch11b-Directly
TEXT:

"I have a plan," I say.

"What in the world?" Daren stares at me, eyes wide. "Where is that aura coming from? Did Eiden stop time again?"

"No," I say, channeling mana into Daren's shield to buy us time. "The device is siphoning the mana leaking from their bones."

"It can do that?" Daren asks, looking down at his blade.

"Apparently," I say. "We're going on the offensive. I have wards for everyone and high-tier spell access. We pressure the necromancer directly to force the stillwaters out of formation."

I point across the square.

"Daren, I'm loading my heavy wards onto you. Draw their fire and hold their attention."

"I can do that," Daren says, planting his boots firmly on the stones.

"Hadrik, charge the necromancer the second we step out. Hit him with everything you've got."

"Consider it done!" Hadrik grins.

I turn to Flower.

"Flower, can you get airborne?"

"With fire jets from my feet," Flower says. "I can hold altitude as long as I need."

"Good. You, Illuna, and I take high ground. We bombard them from above to overwhelm their dome."

"Aye, aye, sir!" Flower salutes.

"And Kate," I say. "Stay inside this perimeter. You have the best range and the cleanest sightlines. Watch for openings while we draw their fire, and punish every mistake."

"Understood," Kate says, ice glinting along her fingers. "I'll make them pay for every slip."

"Brace yourselves," I say, finishing the ward matrix. "Move!"

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Directly2, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Directly2)


ID: Ch11b-Directly2
TEXT:

The moment the wards settle, we break from the dome. Hadrik sprints straight for Zack, closing the gap in heartbeats before the air elementalist hurls him back with a gale blast. Hadrik rolls, recovers, and charges straight back, hammering the support dome with concussive punches.

Daren advances on the five stillwaters. A skeleton looses a searing beam of flame; Daren catches it on his broadsword, absorbing the fire into the enchanted steel and hurling it straight back. The strike breaks against their dome, but Daren holds their undivided fury, parrying spells with blade and shield.

I nod to Flower. She sprints, leaps, and unleashes twin pillars of flame from her bare feet, rocketing thirty feet into the morning sky. I lift behind her on raw updrafts, feeling the clumsy pull of the wind beneath my boots.

We spread thirty paces apart over their dome, raining fire, water, and stone upon their barrier from above.

"No, idiot! You're doing it all wrong!" Arraka's voice screeches from the amulet below. "Stop hammering the entire perimeter and strike a single point! That support mage is pitifully slow at reinforcing localized punctures—he never learned concentrated mana distribution! I told him six hundred years ago, and the moron didn't believe me until I punched a tree branch clean through his ribs!"

Taking tactical advice from Arraka grates, but looking at the shimmering dome, I see the truth in her insult. The energy is spread wide across the sphere—resilient against broad area blasts, but paper-thin against a concentrated strike.

I channel a concentrated spear of lightning between my palms while hovering in place. When the voltage hums so violently the air turns to ozone, I line up the shot and fire straight at the support mage's skull.

The lightning spear punches through the barrier like a needle through parchment, blowing the support mage's skull into scorched splinters.

The dome shatters. The remaining four skeletons freeze in sudden confusion.

I seize the window. Pouring a massive surge of mana into the street, I transmute the cobblestones beneath them into a bubbling, steaming pool of corrosive acid.

The skeletons sink into the green slurry, their lower limbs dissolving on contact. The air elementalist conjures a panicked updraft to lift his upper torso out of the vat, but I clap my hands, slamming two granite slabs together to crush his skull to dust. The others submerge completely, their bones hissing and melting into sludge.

I drop back to the pavement as my device's power fades to zero. Blood drips from my nose, my hands shake violently from mana strain, and the acid pool dissolves into harmless mist, leaving Zack the necromancer standing alone in the ruins of the square.

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Sacrificial, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Sacrificial)


ID: Ch11b-Sacrificial
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_strategy != 3 && v_ch11_strategy != 4) {
"No!" Zack screams, falling to his knees among the shattered cobblestones as his summoned stillwaters melt into foul green vapor. "I won't let it end like this!"

He yanks a curved ritual dagger from beneath his robes and plunges the blackened steel straight into his own chest. A blinding crimson glow erupts from the wound, searing his flesh and robes to cinder.

"The God of Death... shall have his prize..." he rasps, and collapses into a smoking mound of black ash.

Hadrik strides over, looking down at the smoldering pile with a booming laugh.

"As far as dying words go, I've heard better!"

"That wasn't empty theatrics," Illuna says, scanning the square with cold urgency. "A sacrificial death curse. Magic fueled by the caster's lifeblood to purchase effects impossible through standard channels. The ogre shaman hit me with the same foul weave before the fort. This isn't over."

"Relax," Hadrik snorts. "If five living stillwaters couldn't put us down, what's a pile of soot going to do?"

}
#if(v_ch11_strategy == 3 || v_ch11_strategy == 4) {
"No!" Zack screams as his defensive dome shatters and the summoned stillwaters melt into foul green vapor. "I won't let it end like this!"

He yanks a curved ritual dagger from beneath his robes and plunges the blackened steel straight into his own chest. A blinding crimson glow erupts from the wound, searing his flesh and robes to cinder.

"The God of Death... shall have his prize..." he rasps, and collapses into a smoking mound of black ash.

Hadrik strides over, looking down at the smoldering pile with a booming laugh.

"As far as dying words go, I've heard better!"

"That wasn't empty theatrics," Illuna says, scanning the square with cold urgency. "A sacrificial death curse. Magic fueled by the caster's lifeblood to purchase effects impossible through standard channels. The ogre shaman hit me with the same foul weave before the fort. This isn't over."

"Relax," Hadrik snorts. "If five living stillwaters couldn't put us down, what's a pile of soot going to do?"

}
"Tough words from someone who couldn't spot a massive structural hole in a six-hundred-year-old barrier," Arraka snarls from the amulet. "You'd all be corpses without my coaching. I saved every pathetic one of you. Where's my reward?"

"Right here!" Hadrik snatches the gold amulet off Flower's neck and starts whipping it around in rapid circles by the chain.

"Hadrik, stop!" Flower protests. "I told you, she gets motion sickness!"

"Does she?" Hadrik grins, spinning the chain faster. "Must have slipped my mind."

choice(""Put her down, Hadrik. It's not worth the bother."", Ch11b-Bother, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Bother)
choice(""I suppose she does deserve this after all she's done."", Ch11b-Deserve, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Deserve)
choice(""If you're making a point, make it once. A second spin is just entertainment."", Ch11b-Spin, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Spin)


ID: Ch11b-Bother
TEXT:

"Put her down, Hadrik. It's not worth the bother."

"Nonsense, Barry!" Hadrik laughs, keeping the golden chain whipping in a blur. "This is prime entertainment. I might make it part of my daily morning routine. Though I should check with our guest first—wouldn't want to be rude. What do you say, Arraka? Does ten in the morning work for your daily spin?"

"Dwarf!" Arraka's shrieking voice wobbles with nausea from the blur of gold. "When I get out, I'm reaching down your gullet, turning you inside out, and hanging you from an oak by your own intestines as a tree swing!"

"So ten is too early?" Hadrik chuckles, grinning down at the blurred locket. "Should we schedule for eleven instead?"

choice("Continue", Ch11b-Hole, v_current_scene = Ch11b-Hole)


